{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conditional proposition conditional proposition","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md","fileName":"Conditional Proposition.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conditional Proposition","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Formal Logic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Formal Logic","Logically Equivalent","Proof by Contrapositive","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conditional proposition conditional proposition","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md","fileName":"Conditional Proposition.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conditional Proposition","heading":"Conditional Proposition","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The conditional proposition p → q states that if p is true, then q is true. It is false only when its antecedent p is true and its consequent q is false, and it is [[Logically Equivalent]] to ¬p ∨ q.\n\nIts converse q → p is generally different, while its contrapositive ¬q → ¬p is equivalent to the original conditional. This equivalence supports [[Proof by Contrapositive]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Formal Logic","Logically Equivalent","Proof by Contrapositive","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conditional Proposition.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conditional proposition c